 @tylerharris9847  No issues with anything other than AC series, it gets green flickering and sometimes the PC turns off.  All other games and apps run fine.

I tried all the known good drivers including 2019 good versions with DDU every time, tried not installing the Adrenalin control panel and drivers only, updated chipset and bios, tried to under clock it and also tested max OC with slider all the way + max power limit (no crash in any app or furmark)just to rule out that it wasn't a bad bin and too close to the edge already but its not. Changed PSU etc.

The only thing that works is if you restart the PC before playing AC games it's fine, only craps out if it was running other games/apps before hand, but those other apps/games work perfect.   I think it's just a ubisoft/AMD incompatibility or something like that? There is a big reddit thread on this very problem where unisoft said they would fix it but they haven't done anything for almost a year.

 @shadowbanned4051  Easy example, PC's have had SSD's for years with practically no discernible gaming difference between a $40 SSD or a $750 SSD. PS5 introduces superfast SSDs and both Nvidia and AMD immediately come out and announce a similar solution for PC. 

We've had mainstream cheap access to 8 core CPUs for nearly a decade and still see practically zero optimisation for anything past 6 cores and the only reason we seen that jump is because the Ps4/One had some to play with. 

Neither Nvidia or AMD have even made gaming centric PC architectures in a long time because there's little point if engines aren't going to make use of them.

 @Alovon  That's like using "optimised settings" that are almost identical to pure ultra and test AMD cards like that while using pure ultra settings on Nvidia and say it "looks almost the same"
Or how about using an i5 to test Nvidia cards because it's better value for money when compared to an R9?
Notice how Steve didn't throw in SAM until the end which gave a nice boost to games either. If anything that would be thrown in the mix faster than DLSS since its not a trade-off between visuals to performance.

 @xhydrox  "2x better in raytracing" 😂 you're talking a lot of shit here. 20% isn't 200% idiot. Better talk less and let the people who actually know things and watched the videos do the talking. 

DLSS will be matched by AMD soon, it is what they said. Both RT and DLSS aren't that important as only a few games support it and not everyone is playing those games. A lot of people even do and don't care about those features. So don't hype it. For example COD players always turn off raytracing anyway, it's a fps eater. Same goes for BF5 and Fortnite. 

What is important is high 1440p and 4K perf and this is what the new Radeon GPUs have. For streamers there is also the relevance, maybe, to have NVENC, AMDs equivalent has worse quality, but at 1080p streaming it is still good enough - just not at 720p.

 @Dr.WhetFarts  What are you talking about... How on earth is the 1080ti low-end when it's literally dead mid-end in all benchmarks?

More on par with 2070 S/2080 than 2060 S, which lags at least 10%. In some games even beating the 2070 S/2080.

My point is that it's absolutely decent for 1440p 60fps as the performance throughout the years held pretty strong.

 @androsforever500 .. Yup! Totally agree, I happen to really like it myself! I have a 2070 Super FE with a G-sync 27" IPS monitor and was happy with the performance I got in Control and Metro Exodus at 1440p/144hz.
I tried playing without Raytracing & Dlls and with it. Besides the fps hit in using it, I liked the rt/dlss version better in both games with smooth game play, no stuttering or tearing.
In singleplayer game situations it's 👍

 @adip1704  Godfall and FLight simulator already use 12gb at 4k ultra though. And ray tracing will only make them consume more VRAM in the future. 
And just because you can only see a few games benchmarked here, doesn't mean Steve cherry picked them. 
Also 50$ more expensive, yes on paper. Good luck buying an RTX3080 at msrp price, in 2023 ;)

 @adip1704  You're sounding more like a person who wants to justify their 3080 buying decision and refuses to acknowledge AMD's positives (even though you yourself asked of the objective positive side of this Radeon card) just to sleep better at night. 
It's not called FUTURE proofing if you only took current games already released as consideration. The point is VRAM usage will never go down, and even if the 16gb of the 6800 cards will only get proper utilization at 2025, it's still called better future proofing. With these, you won't even need to speculate about VRAM usage anymore, just play hard and sleep like a log at night, won't that be nice? 
Also it's still too early to judge availability now, they both sucks though

 @eazen  Thats speculation. If what you say is true then its arguable Nvidia could face a lawsuit.

I.e they purposefully released a gpu with not enough vram so you have to upgrade in a few years. So far people with 3080s have zero probs with ten gb of vram.

Yeah am with most here the 6800xt bit lacklustre at 4k and for the price diff which isnt much i'd rather have nvidia.

 @eazen  And it ain't a problem in any other game? Well maybe we are just (un)lucky to only see 1 game with that problem in those 18 games. 
Though if a driver got corrupted you need a clean install to fix the problem. Just retesting won't fix it.
At the end of the day it doesn't matter anyway. Nobody is gonna buy a 2060 for 4k gameplay.

 @nofreebeer  Dont decades go from 0 to 9 ?
Ie. we started counting years (and thus decades) in our current system in year 0, so the first 10 years/decade ended when year 9 ended/year 10 started. So we already are in the new decade and 2029 (or rather 01.01.2030) is the start of the next decade.
Otherwise, the millenium would have started 2001, not 2000 and the first decade would have 11 years.

 @formulaic78  so Is life 

Nvida have always done this

Everyone who bought a 1080 early then the 1080 Ti came out for not much more it's just business

 @namenotfound8747  Dude, it wins against 3080 in 1080 AND 1440p, while being almost the same at 4k. All while being cheaper and using less power. DLSS is a gimmick, but if you like gimmicks, AMD has SAM which is imo a better gimmick that gives a nice boost to fps in certain titles.

Are you fnin blind, what compromises are you talking about ??

Are you willing to spend $1500 on 3090 just for some extra frames on 4k ?? Seriously ?

 @makisekurisu4674  lol DLSS.
Dont tell anyone, but a mediocre sharpening filter and a lower than native render resolution aint rocket science.

Kinda surprised you didnt also bring up how awefully these cards run games with hairworks.

 @hyprmetlfan123  I am really wondering if AMD could actually compete with DLSS 2.0. It's seriously impressive, though for a small number of games.
If AMD could do the AMD thing like they did with freesync it would be a miracle IMO.
BUT, I absolutely hope that happens.

 @makisekurisu4674  Tell me, if raytracing is a gimmick like you say, then why are there over 20 upcoming games in production that'll feature ray-tracing?
I honestly can say, especially with the consoles and AMD cards now having raytracing and the way that game devs love nothing more but to try and get one over the competition in the graphics department, that AAA games featuring ray-tracing will become very common within the next 18 months or so (most AAA games released will have it). Also, when it comes to raytraced games on PC, you'll always see DLSS tagged onto them as well.
Unless you're one of those people that upgrade their card each and every generation, then I'd say that raytracing performance (and DLSS) should play a part in the decision that you make, if your planning on buying a card this generation, especially if you're in it for the long run (ie 3 years +)

 @shiraz1736  I think it's not BS. I really want it to succeed. It may be unusable now but I think it will work very well in 1-2 years time.
But nobody should be any current gpus for RT only. Nvenc and DLSS on the other  hand are genuinely something worth paying the 50$ extra or saving 80$ for the 3070 if it's available.
The 6800 should be 500$ and 6800xt around 599$ for them to be compititive.

 @TheAlbaniaGaming  uhhhhh can you find a 3070? If so is it $500? I'm telling you right now, it's not a $500 card. There's gonna be a rerelease and it's gonna be more expensive

I also said that $400 for a 1080ti is too high as well. The sweet spot is $350. Also if you don't include RT, 2080ti and 1080ti are roughly the same in performance

1080ti is still a good purchase no matter what.

 @denkt  Ah yes, the arguement of 'fanboy'.
I've given out reasons while yours, pretty knowledgable.
Statistics tell, not mere words.

 @legendarymop  the CPU bottleneck is benefitting Nvidia not AMD. The Ampere is like Fury X and has a lot of cores and a lot of them are going wasted at low resolution. Watch Gamers Nexus 3080 review Ampere will have identical performance in 1080p and 1440p and in 4k it performs way better.  
Navi has like half the stream processors but can be fed more efficiently.  
Navi will be superior ultra high fps gaming card in 1080p and 1440p while Ampere will have an edge at 4k and beyond.

 @railshot888  Not sure I fully get your comment, on hardware unboxed`s review the 6800xt was outperforming a rtx3090 @1080p and the 3080 at 1440p, but loses to both in 4k on average over 18 games.

Interestingly the 6800xt doesn't loose by very much at 4k against the rtx3080 though, but wins big in 1080p and 1440p on average.

I definitely agree with the middle and last part of your comment though, the tables has turned regarding the focus point of the architectures.

 @HaitiSpaceAgency  I meant for your CPU. I'll probably stick with the 3900X too. GPU is a no brainer.

It's a tough choice this time. AMD seems to win at 1440p gaming, but lose at 4K and productivity stuff. The main thing that's going to hurt them I think is not having stuff like ShadowPlay, GeForce Voice, etc.

For me I don't stream or anything, and I enjoy Linux, so AMD is probably the safer bet, but if I was streaming or something Nvidia's encoder seems way better. Hopefully as more next gen games come out more and more games will get optimizations for AMD since the consoles are using it.

 @AlexanTheMan  so you're just going to ignore what I said? What are you on rn. I said you cannot use the exception as the rule. The 3090 has 24GB of VRAM and is for ML, the 6900XT is a pure gaming card. The 3090 is just for shits and giggles at Nvidia, for AMD, it is not.

When you compare the RULE, not the EXCEPTION, you see that the 3080 and 6800XT are similarly priced, and 3070 and 6800 are less similar but still close.

This is called facing reality. These are the prices. You fleeing to exceptions doesn't help your case, it hurts it. It pains me to explain basic logical fallacies to you
